from common import *
it('KERNEL-0094','interface','3.3','interface KernelTx { key: ArenaKey (a PlatKey or a transient arena TransientKey, §3.4); level: Level; ops: KernelOp[] (declared form only: independent ops, applied in canonical order); expect: Invariant[]; onFail: reject | repair | downgrade; batch: BatchScope; stage: StageId (the stage whose code issues the batch); rederivesFrom: StageId (earliest stage the batch re-derives: = stage in generation; the start of the edit re-derivation for an edit (§7.3); the first stage of §4.1 order for a warp or smoothing batch, which keeps nothing (§3.6; J-K33)); clearRegion?: Ring[] (coarse batch into finished fabric and its preparation batches); prepared?: VertexRef[] (with clearRegion: vertices earlier batches of the same edit or stage placed for this batch contacts with finer geometry, the only finer vertices it may meet, §3.6 candidate (c)); workLimit: number (operations, from the stage declaration §4.1, §14) }.',['§3.3 code block'],'kernel',['§4.1 stage declaration (workLimit, level, stage rank)','§14 tx interface','§7.3 edit re-derivation start'],['every stage (§4)','every edit (§7)','@batch','@clearRegion','@prepared','@workLimit'],['KernelTx','batch','rederivesFrom','onFail','expect'])
it('KERNEL-0095','type','3.3','type BatchScope = { kind: stage; stage } | { kind: district; id } | { kind: block; id } | { kind: edit; edit: EditId } | { kind: entity; id } (a transient arena batches: the entity whose derivation opened it).',['§3.3 code block'],'kernel',[],['@KernelTx','@transientArena'],['BatchScope'])
it('KERNEL-0096','interface','3.3','interface CommitRecord (returned by every commit; kernel-internal slots, never persisted): version; key; level; faces per level >= batch level {created, changed, removed} (the dirty set); edgesAdded; edgesRemoved; steinerAdded; steinerDissolved; moved: VertexId[] (vertices a warp or smoothing moved, Steiner re-seats included - their incident edges are §3.10 moved edges, GEO-35); promoted: VertexId[] (finer vertices whose birth level the batch lowered, §2.2 rule 3 - contacts, prepared vertices, stretch ends; the edit undo record keeps their lattice points, former levels and flags and the anchor contact names promotion stripped, §7.4); demoted: { vertices; edges (one half-edge per pair) } (birth levels raised; undo keeps former levels); unflagged: VertexId[] (split flag or kernel pin cleared other than by moving - prepared contact or stretch end promotion, a broken run, a pin released by §2.2 rule 3 (b), an unmoved vertex beside which a move displaced its run; undo restores flags); owners: Id[]; roles: RoleKey[] ((role, entity, ordinal) keys whose chains changed); warnings; work.',['§3.3 code block','§3.3 The commit record bullet'],'kernel',['@batch'],['@invariants (batch-local)','@indexRebuild','§8 display-list dirty set','§6.2 digest update','§7.4 undo record'],['CommitRecord','dirty set','unflagged'])
it('KERNEL-0097','rule','3.3','Batch granularity: a transaction batch is one growth stage, one district infill, one block plot subdivision or one edit; every insertion is part of a batch.',['§3.3 prose after code'],'kernel',['@KernelTx'],['§4 stages','§7 edits'],['batch granularity'])
it('KERNEL-0098','rule','3.3','Transient arena batches use this protocol with level 0 (its one level); batch { kind: entity } naming the entity whose derivation opened it; stage and rederivesFrom both the stage id under which the opening rung draws its streams (§6.1), so no face on it is kept content; no clearRegion or prepared.',['§3.3 prose after code','§3.4 transient arenas'],'kernel',['@transientArena','§6.1'],['@transientArena ops'],['transient arena batch'])
it('KERNEL-0099','mechanism','3.3','Batch execution: the kernel applies the ops to a scratch overlay, serves every query inside the batch from the level dynamic grid plus the static partitions, validates the batch-local invariants on the touched faces and their one-ring (§3.10), and commits or rejects atomically. Local repair (§5) runs inside the transaction on the overlay; for a batch an edit dry run has already validated, see §5.3.',['§3.3 prose after code'],'kernel',['@KernelTx','@queries','@invariants'],['@CommitRecord','§5 local repair','§5.3'],['scratch overlay','atomic commit'])
it('KERNEL-0100','rule','3.3','One level per transaction: a batch creates vertices, edges, owners and roles of its own level only, plus the Steiner vertices, promotions and demotions of §2.2 rule 3; it may clear finer content inside its faces (clearFiner, subject to kept content); a warp batch moves vertices of every level >= its own (§3.6); anything else is E_LEVEL. A lane a finer stage would add to a coarser level is a separate coarser transaction (§4.7); a stage writing two levels (S3: carvings L0, reservations L1) issues one transaction per level in §4.1 batch-table order.',['§3.3 bullet One level per transaction'],'kernel',['§2.2 rule 3','§4.1 batch table'],['@E_LEVEL','§4.7','§4.2 S3'],['one level per transaction'])
it('KERNEL-0101','rule','3.3','Kept content (J-K14): every face records the rank of the stage whose batch created it (LevelFaces.stage, §2.2; rank = the stage position in §4.1 topological order, fixed per generator version). A batch clears finer content inside its faces only where that content stage rank >= that of rederivesFrom; finer faces an earlier stage made are KEPT - neither cleared nor re-owned - and their edges and vertices are obstacles: an op crossing a kept edge, splitting a kept face or bringing new geometry within attractCm of it returns E_KEPT, except where §3.6 candidate (b) attracts onto and promotes a finer vertex on a route the chain ends on or joins.',['§3.3 bullet Kept content'],'kernel',['§2.2 LevelFaces.stage','§4.1 topological order','@rederivesFrom'],['@E_KEPT','§7.3 re-derivation','§13.2 stage property tests'],['kept content','keptContent','J-K14','stage rank'])
it('KERNEL-0102','rule','3.3','E_KEPT governs insertion and clearing only; a move keeps nothing (§3.6). The re-walk re-parents each kept face into the new face containing it (a kept face never straddles a new edge because crossing it is refused), so nesting holds.',['§3.3 bullet Kept content'],'kernel',['@keptContent'],['@reparent','@moveValidation'],['reparent','re-walk'])
it('KERNEL-0103','rule','3.3','In generation E_KEPT cannot arise on a correct pipeline because the stage order builds no finer face before a coarser stage could meet it (wall band parts §4.7 S10 (0), §4.8; hinterland L3 content §4.9); the rule turns a wrong stage domain into a typed failure caught by the stage property tests (§13.2). In an edit it returns to the dry run as a conflict (§7.1). Wall and margin bands rebuilt in a re-derivation scope never write the other side: §7.3 item 2. clearFiner never removes kept content and never reports it as a conflict; only locked or pinned content is a conflict (§2.2 rule 6).',['§3.3 bullet Kept content'],'kernel',['@keptContent','§4.1 stage order'],['§13.2 stage property tests','§7.1 dry run','§7.3 item 2','§2.2 rule 6'],['clearFiner','E_KEPT in generation'])
it('KERNEL-0104','rule','3.3','A batch faces are the faces it names or creates. A same-level split of an edge those faces share with outside faces - the neighbour step kerb split beside finished plots and footprints (II.1 (3)) - adds that vertex to the outside faces rings; they are not the batch kept content, neither cleared nor re-owned, and each ring equals its prior ring with the vertex removed (the seam exception, §4.6 step 7, §7.3 item 1).',['§3.3 bullet A batch faces'],'kernel',['II.1 (3) neighbour step'],['§4.6 step 7','§7.3 item 1','V01 neighbourhoods'],['seam exception','batch faces'])
it('KERNEL-0105','mechanism','3.3','Clear regions and prepared contacts (LC-7; J-I10, J-I12, J-I13, J-K24): a coarse batch inserted into finished fabric - §4.11 carve of a canal, moat or basin, §4.4 outer moat, S1 water operator re-carving a reach (§7.3 (b)), an epoch frame growth (§3.2 (9)) - runs as a declared sequence of single-level batches sharing one CLEAR REGION (KernelTx.clearRegion), because under the plain rule one canal would clear the town (an L0 face is a whole land component).',['§3.3 bullet Clear regions'],'kernel',['@KernelTx'],['§4.11 carve','§4.4 outer moat','§7.3 (b) water operator','@frameGrowth','§13.1-§13.2 carve fixtures'],['clearRegion','clear region','declaredSequence','LC-7'])
it('KERNEL-0106','term','3.3','Defined term FOOTPRINT (of an edit, the clear region): computed by the operator in its dry run (§4.11 carve and outer moat; §7.3 (b) 2-4 re-carved reach; for frame growth the whole L1 faces §3.2 (9) names instead): its new chains buffered (buffer, §3.7) by attractCm plus the clearance of the chain class (a channel: its bank setback), UNITED with every L1 cell the edit way rule touches - cells that buffer meets, cells bordering a stretch the edit removes or prunes or a kept way replaced stretch, cells a re-routed way crosses, and where the edit re-traces a wall run the cells bordering the old run or the strip-line stretches it removes and the cells the re-traced run new strips will take. The footprint is exactly the union of those cells, since the buffer lies inside the cells it meets.',['§3.3 bullet Clear regions'],'kernel (operators own the computation: §4.11, §7.3 (b))',['@buffer','@attractCm','class clearance / bank setback (§4 tables)'],['@clearRegion rules 1-3'],['footprint'])
it('KERNEL-0107','step','3.3','Clear region rule 1 Clearing: clearFiner in a batch declaring a clear region removes each finer face whose interior meets the region - whole, never part - with the partition edges and vertices no remaining face uses, nothing else; locked and pinned owners return as conflicts; every finer face not meeting the region is kept content whatever its stage rank. Structural finer edges inside the region (ways, walls, setback and glacis lines, deck spans) are kept unless a preceding batch of the same edit removed them: clearFiner removes partition content only, and preparation (rule 2) re-lays or cuts every structural edge the coarse chain would meet (§4.11 batch (1), §7.3 (b) 3). The coarse batch itself clears nothing; it only splits the finer faces meeting the region (rule 3).',['§3.3 Clear regions rule 1'],'kernel',['@clearRegion'],['@frameGrowth (a)','§4.11 batch (1)','§7.3 (b) 3'],['clearFiner with region'])
it('KERNEL-0108','step','3.3','Clear region rule 2 Preparation: one or more finer-level batches, issued first in the same edit or stage, clear the region (rule 1) and place EVERY point where the coarse chain will meet finer geometry as a vertex named by role (§2.2 rule 7): (i) on each way kept across the new water, a deck two bank vertices carrying (deck_end, bridge, 0|1) under a fresh Bridge, the way stretch between them one straight edge (the deck span); (ii) on each wall run re-traced across the new water (S1 water operator §7.3 (b) 3; a carve crossing a wall §4.11; J-I13), its water gate two bank vertices carrying (gate_end, gate, 0|1) under a fresh Gate, which the Gate record built afterwards names (§2.7); (iii) on the map border line (§3.2 (9)), which must stay closed, its two bank points as anchor contact names, the border stretch between them (one straight edge, or several where the water takes a frame corner or an earlier edit vertex) left across the water unchanged, as a deck span is (the arena outer ring is L0, so new water meets it at its own level); (iv) on every other finer non-way edge the water crosses (settlement-perimeter or other district-boundary stretch, a wall strip setback or glacis line) its two bank points as anchor contact names, the stretch between them removed.',['§3.3 Clear regions rule 2'],'kernel',['§2.2 rule 7 roles','§2.7 Bridge, Gate records','@splitThrough'],['@clearRegion rule 3','§4.11','§7.3 (b) 3'],['preparation','deck_end','gate_end','anchor contact name'])
it('KERNEL-0109','rule','3.3','Contact names: a contact name is placed by splitThrough with its row given without an ordinal; the operator names the entity by §2.2 rule 7 and the kernel allocates that entity smallest free anchor ordinal - from 2 on an enceinte, whose 0 and 1 name an open trace ends - as the op applies, in the batch canonical op order, so no two vertices carry one anchor key (§3.10 one-vertex-per-ordinal check); the name lives until the coarse batch strips it (rule 3).',['§3.3 Clear regions rule 2'],'kernel',['@splitThrough','§2.2 rule 7','@canonicalOrder'],['@invariants one-vertex-per-ordinal','@clearRegion rule 3'],['anchor ordinal','enceinte'])
it('KERNEL-0110','rule','3.3','Preparation cut-backs: a coarse chain never shares a finer edge, so a finer edge the new water would run along is cut back by the preparation like any lesser way; a way cut back beyond its bank setback - every lesser way, and a quay or landing, which the operator extends to the new bank only once the water exists, its end an ordinary Steiner vertex on the L0 bank (§4.11 batch (3)) - leaves no contact and needs no prepared vertex. Every stretch the preparation removes (cut stretch, pruned remainder, kept way replaced stretch, stretch between two contact names) is removed through a StretchRef (§3.4); a way left in two parts is first split by retypeEdges rekey on its far part, so role consistency (§3.10) holds after the batch.',['§3.3 Clear regions rule 2'],'kernel',['@StretchRef','@retypeEdges'],['§4.11 batch (3)','@invariants role consistency'],['cut back','rekey'])
it('KERNEL-0111','step','3.3','Clear region rule 3 Contact: the coarse batch splits the finer faces that meet the region (records re-walked, keeping owners until a later batch of the operator re-owns them), lists the prepared vertices in KernelTx.prepared and may meet finer edges and vertices only there: §3.6 candidate (c) attracts onto each prepared vertex and promotes it. The finer edges at a prepared vertex stay (kept outside the region, the preparation own inside it), so each kept way straight L1 edge between its two promoted bank vertices lies across the new water as the Bridge deck span carrying deck beside way (§2.2 rule 7); each re-traced run edge between its two gate_end vertices is its water gate; the border stretch between its two contact points is the closed frame: no L0 edge crosses the water, no deck splits the water body L0 faces, the way stays one chain through every batch (the structure S1, S3 and S4 build at generation, J-I12). Any other contact with finer geometry is E_LEVEL inside the region and E_KEPT outside it and returns to the dry run (in generation, to the stage property tests). A carve costs its footprint, not the town (re-derivation: §4.11 step 4, §7.3 (b) 8).',['§3.3 Clear regions rule 3'],'kernel',['@prepared','@attraction candidate (c)'],['§4.11 step 4','§7.3 (b) 8','§2.2 rule 7 deck role'],['contact rule','deck span','water gate'])
it('KERNEL-0112','test','3.3','The carve fixtures of §13.1-§13.2 confirm the clear-region rules (milestone M3a).',['§3.3 Clear regions after rule 3'],'§13 testing',['@clearRegion'],['§16 M3a'],['carve fixtures'])
it('KERNEL-0113','fallback','3.3','Pre-decided fallback if a carve fixture cannot be met: the batch clears the finer content of its whole L0 land face and re-derives it (the plain rule - correct, slower); moat: all is then carved only before S5c of the outer stage (where L1 holds only arterials), and an edit carve becomes a declared town-wide re-derivation with the fate report (§7.3).',['§3.3 Clear regions after rule 3'],'kernel',['@clearRegion'],['§4.4 moat all','§7.3 fate report'],['carve fallback'])
add(I)
